A||||25||merlin||What’s the biggest pain in the a$$ repair for stock Fieros||||8||
Z||000000||merlin||12-05-2000||08:45 PM||||For all those that maintain their own Fieros, what do you consider the number one kick-the-car, throw tools across the garage, pain in the butt repair.<P> For me, it’s definitely the damm oil pan gasket on the V6.  Who was the engineer that came up with that design?!  Remove the engine mount and raise the engine?..  What a joke.  I’ve been at it for five hours and just when I think I’m home free the exhaust is still in the way even after removing all the springs.  It looks like I’m going to have to remove the entire exhaust system to get it out…  <P>Does it get worse than this?...<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000001||kinboyatuwo||12-05-2000||08:55 PM||||I think one of the big ones you will hear is clutch!!!!!<BR>am i right guys?<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||4
Z||000002||batboy||12-05-2000||08:56 PM||||Yes it does get worse! Try working on the "blankity blank" clutch! I got so pissed that I sold my manual shift Fiero. Good thing the Batmobile is an auto.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||12-05-2000||09:03 PM||||I haven't done a clutch yet, but the front exhaust manifold was interesting enough.  I removed the entire exhaust system to get more room to work.  That done, it wasn't too bad.||||reg||1
Z||000004||varnster||12-05-2000||09:12 PM||||I just replaced my pan on 87 gt, I took springs off and unbolted pipe from the manifold pipe [two bolts with the springs] <BR>pain in the ass, but once you disconect<BR>those two bolts the pan will drop to the<BR>pavement in a hurry, tip for puting the bolts<BR>back on exhaust compress springs in a vise and use zip ties to keep them compressed<BR>after you get a couple threads in cut the zips off and wrench it tight P.S DONT FORGET<BR>TO CHANGE OUT THE MELFOIL GASKET BETWEEN<BR>THE PIPE. hope this helps if still leaking<BR>look under your distributer I thought it was<BR>the pan but it was the O ring between the <BR>distrib and the block||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrPBody||12-05-2000||09:56 PM||||So far, biggest PITA is replacing the AC compressor.  ('85 4-cylinder)  Similar deal to above: catalytic converter obstructs access.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||12-05-2000||11:22 PM||||My biggest P.I.T.A so far has been the coolant crossover pipe on the V-6. It's <I>under</I> the front manifold. Have to drop the compressor out of the way. Drop the starter. Disconnect a bunch of wiring, -mostly ground wires. The hardest part was actually twisting the thing around so that I could remove it from the car, once I had it unbolted from everything. Probably would have been easier to drop the cat, or the entire exhaust system. Really gave my vocab a workout.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shiner||12-05-2000||11:42 PM||||So far, mine has been the thottle cable.  You have to take MOST of the interior out (glovebox,computer, shift console, shift knob, radio pod, radio, climate controls) and THEN the fun starts.  It took me damn near an hour to wedge my bigass hand to where I could feel both the arm for the throttle and the end of the cable.  It's not really hard, but just a PITA!<P>------------------<BR>-Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT<BR>'86 SE 2M6 (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000008||bubbajoexx||12-06-2000||12:10 AM||||for me it is dignosing clutch prodlems IE master and slave  problems and most of the time it is nether part but the bushings on the clutch fork rod.||||reg||7
Z||000009||Black88GT||12-06-2000||12:19 AM||||Never done it before, but I heard the water pump was a pain.  When I had the 3.4 installed, I made sure I got a water pump!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan87GT||12-06-2000||07:46 AM||||Whith out a doubt  - broken exhaust manifold bolts!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||12-06-2000||09:35 AM||||Now, Dan, you know as well as I do, it's real <B>easy</B> to break exhaust manifold bolts!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Actually, (almost) any job I have to do for the first time, is the biggest pain.  Whoever said "learning is fun," anyway?!  "Having learned" is much more fun!<BR>     <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000012||Wade87GT||12-06-2000||09:40 AM||||Changing the water pump with the engine in the car is really a bear the first time around. You have to access it from the top, through the wheelwell and from the bottom. The bolts are various sizes and lenghts. The hardest part has to be scraping off the old gasket material in such a confined area. But I knew that a clutch job would be insane, so I paid the local Aamco big dollars to do it for me.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Old Lar||12-06-2000||10:18 AM||||Paying someone else to do the repairs.||||reg||13
Z||000014||bHooper||12-06-2000||10:23 AM||||i've done the clutch (a true learning lesson) and i am now doing the suspension.<P>i haven't done the A/C, i need to replace the whole thing.  it looks like a bear, with the engine in, but would be extremly easy with it out.<P>unfortunately, mine is in.  therefore, the A/c may have to wait another year  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000015||1FST2M6||12-06-2000||10:31 AM||||i'd say front manifold bolts on the 6's the clutch isn't that bad.. i've done a few.. and it gets easier everytime.. and i ALWAYS have spare parts.. still not sure why though... ||||reg||1
Z||000016||ThatRickGuy||12-06-2000||11:18 AM||||well, working in a full shop makes a lot of things easier, altho, my distrubuter is currently sitting 180 degrees and I'm not about to pull the damn thing apart to fix it now. I never had a problem with the exhaust tho, hoise her up, a few springs a really long extension and those 2 bolt/springs come right out and the whole thing drops (good time to have a helper). I agree on the coolant cross over pipe, I've battle with that thing before, and the front end work wasn't exactly 'pleasant'. Haven't had the joy of water pump or clutch work so I can't speak for them.||||reg||1
Z||000017||TheFiero64||12-06-2000||11:38 AM||||I hate to replace the fuel pump. I think that is the worst.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||12-06-2000||12:33 PM||||Replacing a valve cover gasket (left/front bank) WITHOUT removing the intake manifolds.||||reg||1
Z||000019||mshill||12-06-2000||12:53 PM||||On both my V6s I have replaced the clutch when the engine was out.  I wouldn't even try to get the back valve cover off without taking off the intake, it's just too frustrating.  Last night I struggled with an oil pan for about 2 hours before I got it off (cleaning/painting and putting it on my 3.4L). I have replaced the fuel pump and didn't think it was too bad.  I would have to say that if the engine was in the car, that the clutch and water pump would be a real pain.||||reg||1
Z||000020||jch82||12-06-2000||12:58 PM||||this one is a small problem but for those that change their own oil, the oil filter is a pain.||||reg||12
Z||000021||MikeE||12-06-2000||06:00 PM||||For me, the clutch was the biggest PITA.  Like an idiot, I figured I could just drop the trans since the manual says you can.  I didn't have the special engine support bracket so I called some dealers and trans shops (to try to rent it).  They said they don't use it and instead, just rely on a pipe across the engine bay.<P>I figured, hey, if the pros do it that way, what the heck.  It didn't occur to me that when putting the trans back, I would have no way to stabilize both the engine and trans while trying to get them to mate.  I guess the shop can call over all the other workers but I finally had to resort to having my wife lever the engine with a big pipe.  Needless to say, the Fiero dropped a few more status points in her eyes and I heard her mumbling "never again" as she went back into the house.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||DougV||12-06-2000||06:47 PM||||Look under the hood of new cars lately?! There are so many covers and such you can't even see the engine! LOL I'll take working on my Fiero any day! BTW- after the first time you drop your engine cradle, the next time is a snap. I hated changing my clutch pedal. My back was KILLING me when I was done!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||theogre||12-06-2000||09:30 PM||||The water pump quit on my 87 4 cyl shortly after I got the car. I sent it out. It's not only a *****  to get on/off but you have to press that fraggin pully because there are 2 differant ones so the pump makers don't supply it.||||reg||1
Z||000024||FIERO1985||12-06-2000||09:33 PM||||Clutch and the intake manifold gasket. Clutch costed me 900 how about all of you guys ||||reg||1
Z||000025||AkursedX||12-07-2000||09:46 AM||||The worst thing I've had to replace so far on my Fiero has been the A/c Belt.  If I would of known it was that difficult, I would of waited till it snapped!  Took me damn near 4 hours from start to finish for one freaking belt!!||||reg||8
